@* Exercise 086 - Render Mode Static Ssr (advanced).
   Goal:   Stay useful when there is no circuit yet - render something that works
           without JavaScript, and do not reach for JavaScript before it exists.
   Drills: branching markup on RendererInfo.IsInteractive, and guarding an interop
           call so a static render cannot make one.
   Passes: dotnet test --filter FullyQualifiedName~Ex086_ *@
<span id="mode">@(RendererInfo.IsInteractive ? "interactive" : "static")</span>

@* TODO: render one of two things, depending on RendererInfo.IsInteractive:
     interactive  <button id="save" @onclick="Save">save</button>
     static       <form id="fallback" method="post" action="/save">
                    <button type="submit">save</button>
                  </form>
   A click handler is dead markup before the circuit attaches, so the static branch
   posts a real form instead - that is the whole point of the row. *@

@code {
    [Inject] public IJSRuntime JS { get; set; } = default!;

    public const string SetupFunction = "ex086.setup";

    public int Saves { get; private set; }

    private void Save() => Saves++;

    // TODO: call JS.InvokeVoidAsync(SetupFunction) once the component is up - but
    // ONLY when the renderer is interactive. A JS call during static rendering has
    // nothing to talk to; in a real app it throws, which is why the guard belongs
    // here rather than in the caller.
    // Note: bUnit runs this hook whatever RendererInfo says, so the guard is the
    // only thing standing between a static render and the call.
    protected override Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
        => throw new NotImplementedException("TODO: Ex086 - set up only when interactive");
}
